    ThreadedHasher.cpp, CheevosIndex.cpp: a game with a hash and no id gets its id from the library, no re-hash (audit #186 PL-08)
    
    The startup index took a game only when its cheevosHash was empty, so a
    game whose hash was on disk and whose id was not never gained one until a
    forced INDEX GAMES read every ROM again. RC-5 left every game indexed in a
    session that ended with a reboot in that state (fork #183): checkCheevosHash
    saved the recovery file with the hash, the id was decided afterwards in
    memory and lost with the reboot. ec2de8ae7 fixed what is written from now
    on and nothing already written -- fixing forward is not enough (blindspot
    10).
    
    The rule moves to CheevosIndex::take, a pure function es-unit-tests holds
    (five cases): no hash, or a forced run, reads the ROM; a hash with an id is
    done; a hash without an id is a lookup. start() collects the lookups apart;
    the constructor, once the hash library has come, queues the ones the
    library knows -- a save with the id, no read of the ROM (no CRC either, in
    a system netplay never asked for) -- and leaves the rest as they were, so
    a library of games without a set costs no card and no toast at every
    startup. A run with nothing but unresolvable lookups shows no card, starts
    no thread and is deleted where it was made. A resolved lookup logs
    "ThreadedHasher: id <n> for [system] name from the hash library, no file
    read".
    
